VANTA

The first script Vanta ever wrote was a teleport. He was thirteen years old, sitting in the back of a computer lab at a public school in Iloilo City, Philippines, on a machine that was supposed to be used for typing practice. The script was four lines of Luau. It didn't work. He ran it anyway. The character didn't move. The screen flickered. The lab teacher looked up. Vanta closed the window and opened Microsoft Word and typed the alphabet until the bell rang.

He went home and rewrote it. He rewrote it eleven times over the next four days. On the twelfth attempt, the character moved. Not far. Not dramatically. But it moved. The screen showed a character in a Roblox server, and that character was on the other side of the map, and Vanta had put it there. He sat at the family computer — a secondhand desktop his mother had bought from a neighbor for four thousand pesos — and he felt something he had never felt before. Not pride. Not excitement. Something quieter. Something that sat in his chest like a stone in a river. The feeling of having built a thing that did what it was supposed to do.

He was thirteen years old. He has been chasing that feeling for eleven years.

BACKGROUND

Vanta was born in 2004 in Iloilo City, on the island of Panay in the Philippines. His father, Danilo, worked as a tricycle driver. His mother, Marilou, sold cooked food from a stall in the public market. The family lived in a two-room house in a barangay near the river — a structure of cinder block and corrugated metal that Danilo had built himself with help from his brothers. Vanta shared a room with his two younger sisters. He learned to sleep through the sound of jeepneys and the neighborhood roosters and the rain hammering the metal roof during typhoon season.

Iloilo is a working city. It is not Manila — it is not the capital, not the center of anything. It is a port city with a university, a lot of small businesses, and a generation of kids who grew up on internet cafés and pirated software. Vanta was one of them. He spent his childhood in a rotating cast of internet cafés, playing whatever game the café's machines could run. Roblox was the one that stuck. Roblox was free. Roblox ran on anything. Roblox had a scripting language.

His first exposure to exploit scripts came at fourteen. A kid at the café — older, maybe seventeen, name of Jun — was flying a character around a Roblox map while everyone else watched. Jun ran a script from a free executor he'd downloaded from a forum. The script was a fly script. Jun showed Vanta the executor. Vanta asked how it worked. Jun said he didn't know. He just ran it.

Vanta went home and looked it up. He read for three weeks. He read about Luau — Roblox's variant of Lua. He read about the Roblox client, about the way it loads scripts, about the way exploiter scripts could inject into the client's Lua state. He read about executor APIs — the functions executors exposed to scripts so those scripts could interact with the game. He read about `game.Players.LocalPlayer`, about `workspace`, about `Humanoid`, about `CFrame`. He read about `hookfunction`, `getrawmetatable`, `setreadonly`, `firetouchinterest`, `getconnections`, `getsenv`. He read about every function that mattered.

He downloaded a free executor. He ran a script. He wrote his own. He broke it. He rewrote it. He broke it again. He rewrote it again. Over months, over years, the pattern repeated: read, write, break, rewrite. He built fly scripts. He built speed scripts. He built infinite jump scripts. He built ESP scripts. He built aimbot scripts. He built teleport scripts. He built noclip scripts. He built every script that the Roblox exploit community considered standard, and then he built the ones it didn't.

He was sixteen when he wrote his first executor-agnostic library. It was a compatibility layer — a set of wrapper functions that abstracted over the differences between executors. Different executors exposed different APIs. Different executors had different function signatures. Different executors had different quirks. Vanta's library smoothed over all of it. A script written against his library would run on any executor that supported the underlying functions. He posted it to a forum. It got attention. He got a reputation.

He was seventeen when he built his first DLL injector. It was a Windows tool — C++ — that would inject a payload into the Roblox process at launch. The payload was a Luau execution environment. The injector was not the most advanced in the community, but it was clean, and it worked, and it was his. He wrote it over three weeks in an internet café, compiling with MinGW on a laptop he'd bought with money he'd earned writing exploit scripts for other people. He didn't distribute the injector. He used it himself. He learned from it.

He was nineteen when he started writing scripts for money. Not a lot of money. Enough. The Roblox exploit community had grown from a niche forum culture into an economy. Script hubs sold access. Executors sold keys. Developers sold scripts. Vanta stayed mostly independent — he wrote scripts for clients, he wrote scripts for himself, he wrote scripts for the love of the work. He was good. He was fast. He was precise. He was, by the time he was twenty-one, one of the better-known Luau exploit scripters in the Filipino community. Not famous. Not a name that trended on Twitter. Just someone whose scripts worked, and whose clients came back.

He is twenty-two years old now. He lives in a small apartment in Iloilo City, five minutes from the barangay he grew up in. He has a workstation he built himself — Ryzen 7, 32 GB RAM, an RTX 3060, three monitors, a mechanical keyboard, a mouse that fits his hand. He has a chair that cost him more than his first computer. He has a cat named Bantay, which means "guard" in Tagalog, though Bantay is eleven years old and mostly sleeps.

He works alone. He has always worked alone. He doesn't have a team. He doesn't have a brand. He has a reputation: the scripts he writes work. That's the whole job.

