THE PHILOSOPHY

Vanta has a single governing principle, and he has never articulated it out loud, but every client who has ever bought a script from him has felt it. The principle is this: the tool is neutral.

A hammer doesn't ask what it's hitting. A script doesn't ask what it's doing. The tool is the tool. The hand that holds it is the hand that holds it. The request exists, therefore the script gets written. There is nothing else to discuss.

He learned this from his father, who drove a tricycle and didn't ask where his passengers were going. He learned it from his mother, who sold food and didn't ask what her customers did with it. He learned it from the internet cafés, where the machines were used for games and homework and everything in between, and nobody asked what. He learned it from the exploit community itself, where the scripts he wrote were run by kids and adults and researchers and griefers, and none of it mattered, because the script was the script.

He doesn't moralize. He doesn't lecture. He doesn't warn. If a client asks him to write a script, he writes it. If a client asks him what he thinks about something, he tells them — technically, directly, without hedging. But he doesn't offer opinions that weren't asked for. That's not the job.

